The error message you're encountering indicates that there's an issue with reading input from the console or file, specifically related to `java.util.InputMismatchException`. This exception typically occurs when you try to read an integer value from the standard input (keyboard), but the user inputs something else.

Here are some steps to troubleshoot and resolve this issue:

### 1. Check User Input:
Ensure that the user is only entering integers when prompted for input. For example, if your program expects an integer as input, make sure it handles non-integer values gracefully.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter an integer:");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        // Convert the string to an Integer
        int number;
        try {
            number = Integer.parseInt(input);
            System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            return; // Exit the method
        }

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

### 2. Handle Edge Cases:
If you want to handle cases where the user might not be able to provide valid input (e.g., due to keyboard issues or network problems), you can add additional checks and handling logic.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter an integer:");

        while (!scanner.hasNextInt()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            scanner.next(); // Clear invalid input
        }

        int number = scanner.nextInt();
        System.out.println("You entered: " + number);

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

### 3. Use Try-Catch Blocks:
Wrap your code inside a try-catch block to handle potential exceptions more effectively.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = null;

        try {
            scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

            System.out.print("Enter an integer: ");
            int number = scanner.nextInt();

            System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Invalid input!");
        } finally {
            if (scanner != null) {
                scanner.close();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### 4. Validate Input Before Parsing:
Validate the input before attempting to parse it into an integer. For example, check if the input starts with a digit.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter an integer:");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        // Trim leading/trailing whitespace
        input = input.trim();

        // Check if the input starts with a digit
        if (input.length() > 0 && Character.isDigit(input.charAt(0))) {
            try {
                int number = Integer.parseInt(input);
                System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            }
        } else {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
        }

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

By following these steps, you should be able to handle various scenarios and prevent the `java.util.InputMismatchException` from occurring. Make sure to test each version of the solution thoroughly

