Creating an HTML page that displays the background image of a user's computer can be done using JavaScript and the `window` object. Here is a simple example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Background Image Display</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            height: 100vh;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
            transition: background-image 1s ease-in-out;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <button id="displayImage">Display Background Image</button>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('displayImage').addEventListener('click', function() {
            const img = new Image();
            img.src = window.screenCapture.capture();

            // Set the captured image as the background image
            document.body.style.backgroundImage = 'url(' + img.src + ')';
        });
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**:
   - A `<button>` element with the ID "displayImage".

2. **CSS Styling**:
   - The body is styled to take up the full viewport (`height: 100vh`) while centered.
   - It transitions the background color smoothly.

3. **JavaScript Functionality**:
   - When the button is clicked, it captures the screen (which includes the background) using `window.screenCapture`.
   - An `Image` object is created from this capture data.
   - The background image of the entire body is set to the URL of the captured image.

This will make your webpage temporarily display a snapshot of the background of your device whenever you click the button. Note that capturing the screen may not work in all browsers or environments due to security restrictions.

